  {
   "id": "hrSxC0yrBAAJ",
   "from": "Karen Baab",
   "author": "088e8a47a714e2a7",
   "date": "2017-08-14T17:15:40Z",
   "subject": "Re: [geomorph-r-package] Re: NA values returned for estimate.missing",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Hi Emma,<div><br></div><div>I did as you suggest. The dimensions look correct: 57 3 8. There are 8 individuals with 57 3D landmarks. I created a similar file with different specimens and read it in using readland.nts and it worked fine with the estimate.missing function, so I don&#39;t think that&#39;s the issue. </div><div><br></div><div>I also plotted the original data in Morpheus (which allows missing landmarks) and it looks fine visually. I&#39;d be happy to share the &quot;Homo_MD.nts&quot; file if that&#39;s possible / helpful.</div><div><br>Thanks,</div><div><br>Karen<br><div><br></div><div><br><div class=\"gmail_extra\"><br><div class=\"gmail_quote\"></div></div></div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><div><div class=\"gmail_extra\"><div class=\"gmail_quote\">On Sun, Aug 13, 2017 at 7:37 PM, Emma Sherratt <span dir=\"ltr\">&lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">emma.s...@gmail.com</a>&gt;</span> wrote:<br></div></div></div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><div><div class=\"gmail_extra\"><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0 0 0 .8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\">Dear Karen,<div><br></div><div>I suspect you have used the wrong read function for your NTS file. See here for more details: <a href=\"https://github.com/geomorphR/geomorph/wiki/Reading-NTS-files\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://github.com/geomorphR/geomorph/wiki/Reading-NTS-files&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792486065935000&amp;usg=AOvVaw1JuJCS8VdmPqqbPbZcXk8R\">https://github.com/geomorphR/geomorph/wiki/Reading-NTS-files</a></div><div><br></div><div>Basically, there are two file types, and if you&#39;ve used the wrong one, H will not be formatted correctly. Check the dim() of H to make sure it is a 3D array with dimensions you are expecting.</div><div><br></div><div>Emma</div><span><div><br></div><div><br><br>On Saturday, 12 August 2017 08:43:47 UTC+9:30, <a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">klb...@gmail.com</a>  wrote:<blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><br><div><div><div><div style=\"overflow:auto\"><div style=\"max-height:10000px\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><br>I am using the following code to estimate missing landmarks using the TPS method:<div><br></div><div><div>H&lt;-readland.nts(&quot;Homo_MD.nts&quot;)</div><div>E&lt;-estimate.missing(H, method=&quot;TPS&quot;)</div></div><div><br></div><div>The returned value (E) contains a number of NA values, all in the position of the z coordinate for one specimen. The next specimen in the file is unaffected.</div><div><br></div><div>Anyone know why this might happen?</div><div><br></div><div>Thanks, Karen</div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></blockquote></div></span></div></blockquote></div></div></div></div></div>"
    ],
