    [
     2,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><span style=\"font-family:arial,sans-serif;font-size:12.8px\">Hi Emma,</span><div style=\"font-family:arial,sans-serif;font-size:12.8px\"><br></div><div style=\"font-family:arial,sans-serif;font-size:12.8px\">I did as you suggest. The dimensions look correct: 57 3 8. There are 8 individuals with 57 3D landmarks. I created a similar file with different specimens and read it in using readland.nts and it worked fine with the estimate.missing function, so I don&#39;t think that&#39;s the issue. </div><div style=\"font-family:arial,sans-serif;font-size:12.8px\"><br></div><div style=\"font-family:arial,sans-serif;font-size:12.8px\">I also plotted the original data in Morpheus (which allows missing landmarks) and it looks fine visually. I&#39;d be happy to share the &quot;Homo_MD.nts&quot; file if that&#39;s possible / helpful.</div><div style=\"font-family:arial,sans-serif;font-size:12.8px\"><br></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div style=\"font-family:arial,sans-serif;font-size:12.8px\">What&#39;s particularly strange is that it does in fact estimate the missing landmarks, but in 2D. I dropped the z coordinate (the NA values) and plotted the offending specimen, and it looks fine. It&#39;s just not doing it in 3D</div><div style=\"font-family:arial,sans-serif;font-size:12.8px\"><br>Thanks,</div><div style=\"font-family:arial,sans-serif;font-size:12.8px\"><br>Karen</div></div>"
    ]
   ]
